{"name":"Yellow Mask","hex":"#f6d255","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"yellow","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of sunflower petals and lemons; the color obtained by mixing green and red light, or by subtracting blue from white light; the color evoked by light of wavelength around 580 nm; one of the three primary colors in subtractive color systems.","The middle light in a set of three traffic lights, the lighting of which indicates that drivers should stop short of the intersection if it is safe to do so."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a yellow hue.","Lacking courage."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/yellow"},{"term":"mask","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A cover, or partial cover, for the face, used for disguise or protection.","That which disguises; a pretext or subterfuge."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To cover (the face or something else), in order to conceal the identity or protect against injury; to cover with a mask or visor.","To disguise as something else."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/mask"}],"entries":[{"title":"Mask","blocks":[{"text":"A mask is an object normally worn on the face, typically for protection, disguise, performance, or entertainment, and often employed for rituals and rites. Masks have been used since antiquity for both ceremonial and practical purposes, as well as in the performing arts and for entertainment. They are usually worn on the face, though they may also be positioned elsewhere on the wearer's body for effect."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b6/Ravensburg_Fasnet_Papierkrattler_2005.jpg/330px-Ravensburg_Fasnet_Papierkrattler_2005.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mask"},{"title":"Yellow","blocks":[{"text":"Yellow is a color between green and orange on the spectrum of light. It is evoked by light with a dominant wavelength of roughly 575–585 nm. It is a primary color in subtractive color systems, used in painting or color printing. In the RGB color model, used to create colors on television and computer screens, yellow is a secondary color made by combining red and green at equal intensity. Carotenoids give the characteristic yellow color to autumn leaves, corn, canaries, daffodils, and lemons, as well as egg yolks, buttercups, and bananas. They absorb light energy and protect plants from photo damage in some cases. Sunlight has a slight yellowish hue when the Sun is near the horizon, due to atmospheric scattering of shorter wavelengths."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/Cornwall_Daffodils.jpg/330px-Cornwall_Daffodils.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yellow"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c8/MaskOfAgamemnon.jpg/500px-MaskOfAgamemnon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c8/MaskOfAgamemnon.jpg/1280px-MaskOfAgamemnon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c8/MaskOfAgamemnon.jpg/500px-MaskOfAgamemnon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The so-called 'Mask of Agamemnon', a 16th-century BC mask discovered by Heinrich Schliemann in 1876 at Mycenae, Greece. Displayed at the National Archaeological Museum, Athens.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMaskOfAgamemnon.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e5/Museo_Nacional_de_Antropolog%C3%ADa_-_Wiki_takes_Antropolog%C3%ADa_079.jpg/500px-Museo_Nacional_de_Antropolog%C3%ADa_-_Wiki_takes_Antropolog%C3%ADa_079.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e5/Museo_Nacional_de_Antropolog%C3%ADa_-_Wiki_takes_Antropolog%C3%ADa_079.jpg/1280px-Museo_Nacional_de_Antropolog%C3%ADa_-_Wiki_takes_Antropolog%C3%ADa_079.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e5/Museo_Nacional_de_Antropolog%C3%ADa_-_Wiki_takes_Antropolog%C3%ADa_079.jpg/500px-Museo_Nacional_de_Antropolog%C3%ADa_-_Wiki_takes_Antropolog%C3%ADa_079.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Funeral mask of K'inich Janaab' Pakal at the National Museum of Anthropology (Mexico)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMuseo_Nacional_de_Antropolog%C3%ADa_-_Wiki_takes_Antropolog%C3%ADa_079.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3c/Mosaic_of_the_theatrical_masks_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg/500px-Mosaic_of_the_theatrical_masks_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3c/Mosaic_of_the_theatrical_masks_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg/1280px-Mosaic_of_the_theatrical_masks_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3c/Mosaic_of_the_theatrical_masks_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg/500px-Mosaic_of_the_theatrical_masks_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Theatrical masks of Tragedy and Comedy. Roman mosaic, second century AD.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMosaic_of_the_theatrical_masks_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/76/COLLECTIE_TROPENMUSEUM_Batak_maskerdans_tijdens_een_dodenfeest_TMnr_60045322.jpg/500px-COLLECTIE_TROPENMUSEUM_Batak_maskerdans_tijdens_een_dodenfeest_TMnr_60045322.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campa